open_questions:
  - "Why are Sarah Sherman and her son, John Farney Woodruff Jr., the only wife and child entirely absent from the family Bible? Every other wife's children are recorded there, including those who died in infancy. Their omission, while John's namesake son survived to adulthood, suggests a deliberate choice rather than an oversight."
  - "What is the outcome of John's contest of Ida Emmons Woodruff's will? Trial transcripts are on order from the Colorado State Archives and represent the next major evidentiary development in this research."
  - "What does the Frank E. Emmons probate file in El Paso County, Colorado contain? This is the highest-priority unexplored record target and may illuminate Ida's family, assets, and the contested estate."
  - "What is Annie E. Chaney's true relationship to John Farney Woodruff? She appears in his 1880 household but biological vs. step relationship is unverified by primary source."
  - "Where and when did John marry Annie E. Chaney? The sworn divorce complaint gives 10 October 1876, Shoals, Martin County, Indiana — treated as the better-supported date given its consistency with daughter Edna's proven birth date (28 Aug 1877); a separate state abstract of the divorce record instead gives 1877, likely a clerical error. No marriage record, license, or other independent documentation of the Shoals, Indiana marriage has been located. Given John's now-proven Ohio origins, an Indiana marriage is plausible but remains unconfirmed."
  - "What became of David Henry Woodruff, son of John and Annie? Absent entirely from the family Deaths register and unknown to the family until uncovered by this research — see open questions on Annie E. Chaney's page for full detail."
  - "His 1936 newspaper profile and 1939 obituary both call him 'Captain,' while every official military record gives his rank as 1st Lieutenant — courtesy title, late promotion, or reporting error? A family/researcher account (source not yet relocated) holds it was an honorific earned in the field after the company's actual captain fled during a major engagement on the march to the sea."
  - "The official Civil War service database gives his enlistment rank as 1st Sergeant and dates his commission/company transfer to 28 Jan 1865 — conflicting with his own 1912–13 GAR sketch, which describes enlisting as a private and being commissioned 4 April 1865."
  - "Who are half-brothers George, Ben, and Edward Woodruff, named in John's 1939 obituary? Presumably children of a second marriage of Joshua Woodruff's, not yet documented or dated."
  - "Why does the June 1923 Jackson, Ohio newspaper item describe John's marriage to Jennie Bloat as 'the third venture for the groom' when this page documents six marriages? Possibly reflects only what was locally known in Jackson County, where he'd returned in 1920, rather than his full marital history across Ohio, Colorado, and Wyoming — or possibly reflects what John himself told the reporter."

